      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Verified against current official text: 21 CFR 1306.11(a), (e), (f) and (g) including the hospice notation; 1306.12(b); 1306.05(a); 1306.14(e); 1306.13(b); 1306.23; 1306.04(c)-(d); 1306.07(a)-(d) and (f), whose current (d) (effective 2026-07-09) drops the FDA-approval and 1301.28 conditions, as the section states; M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 23(a)-(e), s. 18(d), (d1/2) and (d3/4), s. 9(b) and s. 19D; 105 CMR 700.001 and 700.010(A) (2/2/2024 edition); 105 CMR 721; and Policy 2024-05. The section reports the do-not-fill-before guidance accurately: the BORIM guideline (adopted 2008 and still posted as Appendix C to Policy 15-05) states that the s. 23 'Date of Issuance' is the Do Not Fill Before date and reports DEA's view that the date cannot be modified verbally. The December 2025 Board of Pharmacy newsletter gives 30 days (Massachusetts prescriber) and 5 days (nonresident prescriber) from the date of issuance or the do-not-fill-before date, as applicable. The section presents both as guidance, not statute, and flags the statutory gap they fill. The partial-fill distinctions (1306.23 sets no condition on who requests; 1306.13(b) permits on its conditions; s. 18(d3/4) requires at the patient's request) and the refusal to split one prescription into several are correct. The Massachusetts overlay on 1306.07(b) is supported by 105 CMR 700.010(A) and the 700.001 sample definition. All 21 practice questions are answerable from the section and none is contradicted. Two Low observations do not mislead a candidate within the declared scope: 247 CMR 9.16(2) electronic separability is not mentioned, and 1306.07(g)-(h) are not acknowledged."
